topics.referenceMarkdown:
**Versioning and the recipe id.** A prompt is code: it goes in version control, it has a changelog,
and every request records the exact recipe that produced it. Without this you cannot answer "did this
regression come from a prompt change or a model change?" — which is the question you will be asked
the first time quality moves.

```ts
export type Recipe = {
  id: string;                    // 'triage@3.2.0' â€” immutable once released
  prefix: string;                // regions 1-3
  prefixHash: string;            // cache key
  model: string;                 // pinned, including the dated snapshot
  params: { temperature: number; maxTokens: number };
  schema: z.ZodTypeAny;
  changelog: string;             // why each version exists
};

export function recipeId(r: Recipe) { return `${r.id}#${r.prefixHash.slice(0, 8)}@${r.model}`; }

// Every request logs this. It is the join key between traces, evals and cost.
logger.info({ recipeId: recipeId(ACTIVE_RECIPE), inputTokens, outputTokens, latencyMs });
```

**Assert the cache prefix in CI.** Because regions 1-3 are the cache prefix, a single stray volatile
value in that region silently destroys the cache hit rate for every request — and it looks like a cost
regression, not a bug. A one-line test catches it, and it belongs in the same PR that touches a prompt.

```ts
// 13.3.4 — the assertion that catches volatile data leaking into the cached prefix
it('keeps the cache prefix stable across renders', () => {
  const first  = cachePrefixHash(render({ message: 'my card was charged twice' }));
  const second = cachePrefixHash(render({ message: 'a completely different ticket' }));
  expect(first).toEqual(second);              // region 1-3 must not depend on region 4
  expect(first).toEqual(CACHE_PREFIX_HASH);    // and it must match the released recipe
});
```

