topics.referenceMarkdown:
Ã°Å¸â€Â´ Advanced Ã‚Â· **Must-know**

**Definition.** Prompt caching (also called prefix caching or automatic prefix caching) lets a provider
reuse computation for a **byte-identical prefix** of a request. Everything before and including the
cached prefix is billed at a heavily discounted rate. The prefix must match exactly, which makes
prompt *structure* a cost-control mechanism.

**Mechanics.**

- You mark the request as cacheable, or the provider does so automatically, up to some limit.
- The provider hashes the prefix and stores the KV computation for a short window (minutes to an hour,
  varying by provider).
- On a hit, you are billed only for the uncached suffix, plus a much lower rate for the cached prefix.
- Cache writes incur a premium on the first call, so caching pays off only when the prefix is reused
  more than once.

**Therefore the design rule is structural: put everything static at the front, and everything volatile
at the back.** Order matters enormously.

```
[ system prompt (static) ][ tool schemas (static) ][ conversation summary (semi-static) ][ retrieved evidence (volatile) ][ user question (volatile) ]
|<---------------------- cacheable prefix -------------------------->|<------------- billed at full rate ------------->|
```

**The failure modes that destroy cache hits.**
1. A timestamp, request id, or user name in the system prompt.
2. Tool schemas regenerated with non-deterministic ordering (JSON object key order).
3. Conversation history appended in the wrong place Ã¢â‚¬â€ history must come *after* the static prefix and
   before the volatile tail, and it grows, so eventually it dominates.
4. Different model or different parameters per request, invalidating the prefix.
5. Rerouting between models, which is a different cache namespace.

```ts
export interface CacheableSegment { text: string; cacheable: boolean; }

// Build a prompt so the prefix is byte-identical across requests for the same feature+tenant+model.
export function buildCacheablePrompt(i: PromptInput): ChatMessage[] {
  // 1. Fully static: system prompt from the versioned registry. NO timestamps, NO ids.
  const system = registry.get(i.promptId).renderSystem();

  // 2. Static per tenant: tenant policy is stable for weeks, so it caches well.
  const tenant = `<tenant_context>\n${stableKeyOrder(i.tenantPolicy)}\n</tenant_context>`;

  // 3. Semi-static: the conversation summary changes rarely and is placed before the tail.
  const summary = i.summary ? `<conversation_summary>\n${i.summary}\n</conversation_summary>` : "";

  // 4. Volatile: evidence, then the question. Always last.
  const tail = [
    `<evidence>\n${i.chunks.map((c) => `<chunk id="${c.id}">\n${c.text}\n</chunk>`).join("\n\n")}\n</evidence>`,
    `<user_input>\n${escape(i.userText)}\n</user_input>`,
  ].join("\n\n");

  // One user message combining tenant + summary + tail keeps a single, stable message boundary.
  return [
    { role: "system", content: system },
    { role: "user", content: [tenant, summary, tail].filter(Boolean).join("\n\n") },
  ];
}

// Stable serialization: JSON key order must be deterministic or the prefix hash changes.
export function stableKeyOrder<T>(obj: T): string {
  return JSON.stringify(sortDeep(obj), null, 0);
}
```

**Where to put conversation history.** A long conversation resent verbatim will eventually dominate the
context and evict your cacheable prefix's value. Two approaches:

- **Keep history *after* the static prefix but before the volatile evidence**, so early turns stay
  cached across a session and only new turns are billed fresh. Cache effectiveness decays as history
  grows, which is the correct behaviour.
- **Summarize old turns** into a stable system-side summary (13.2.8). This keeps the prefix small and
  re-cacheable, at the cost of losing detail.

For multi-turn chat, the practical optimum is usually: static prefix Ã¢â€ â€™ rolling summary Ã¢â€ â€™ last N turns
verbatim Ã¢â€ â€™ evidence Ã¢â€ â€™ question.

**Cache-aware accounting.** Measure the hit rate per prompt position, not just globally. Track
`cache_read_tokens / input_tokens` per feature; a low ratio on a chat feature means your prefix is not
actually stable and you have a structural bug.

```ts
export interface CacheMetrics {
  requests: number;
  hits: number;
  hitRate: number;
  cacheReadTokens: number;
  cacheWriteTokens: number;
  freshInputTokens: number;
  savingsCents: number;
}

export function summarizeCache(records: SpendRecord[], pricing: ModelPricing): CacheMetrics {
  const r = records.length;
  const hits = records.filter((x) => x.cacheHit).length;
  const cacheReadTokens = sum(records.map((x) => x.usage.cachedInputTokens));
  const freshInputTokens = sum(records.map((x) => x.usage.inputTokens - x.usage.cachedInputTokens));
  const naive = records.reduce((a, x) => a + costOf(x.usage, { ...pricing, cachedInputPerMTok: pricing.inputPerMTok }).totalCents, 0);
  const actual = records.reduce((a, x) => a + x.costCents, 0);
  return {
    requests: r, hits, hitRate: r > 0 ? hits / r : 0,
    cacheReadTokens, cacheWriteTokens: 0, freshInputTokens,
    savingsCents: round4(naive - actual),
  };
}
```

**When to use / when not to.** Use caching whenever there is a stable prefix reused across requests Ã¢â‚¬â€
which is nearly always, if you structure prompts correctly. It is close to free money. Skip explicit
cache management for one-off batch jobs with no repeated prefix; there is nothing to reuse.

**Trade-offs.** Caching ties your prompt's byte layout to your bill, so refactors that look harmless
(sorting keys differently, adding a field to the system prompt) have a cost impact. Explicit cache
breakpoints are more precise but add provider-specific complexity; automatic caching is simpler but you
must keep the prefix stable by discipline. Caching also reduces your ability to A/B test freely Ã¢â‚¬â€ you
need to be careful that variant prefixes do not thrash a shared cache.

**Production considerations.**
